<article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">3281</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/s12871-025-03281-x</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Anterior Quadratus lumborum block versus modified perichondrial thoracoabdominal nerve block (m-TAPA) for postoperative analgesia following laparoscopic cholecystectomy: a randomized controlled trial</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><contrib-id contrib-id-type="orcid">http://orcid.org/0000-0003-1471-1340</contrib-id><name><surname>&#x0015e;ehirlio&#x0011f;lu</surname><given-names>Serpil</given-names></name><address><email>drserpilsahin@gmail.com</email></address><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1">1</xref><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><contrib-id contrib-id-type="orcid">http://orcid.org/0000-0002-4229-4903</contrib-id><name><surname>Moralar</surname><given-names>D&#x000f6;nd&#x000fc; Gen&#x000e7;</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><contrib-id contrib-id-type="orcid">http://orcid.org/0000-0001-5138-1606</contrib-id><name><surname>Ozakin</surname><given-names>Oguz</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><contrib-id contrib-id-type="orcid">http://orcid.org/0009-0001-9537-2119</contrib-id><name><surname>Aydemir</surname><given-names>Turan</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><contrib-id contrib-id-type="orcid">http://orcid.org/0009-0000-5275-4769</contrib-id><name><surname>Ergul</surname><given-names>Fatma</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1">1</xref></contrib><aff id="Aff1"><label>1</label><institution-wrap><institution-id institution-id-type="ROR">https://ror.org/00nwc4v84</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="GRID">grid.414850.c</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="ISNI">0000 0004 0642 8921</institution-id><institution>Department of Anesthesiology and Reanimation, </institution><institution>Health Sciences University Gaziosmanpa&#x0015f;a Training and Research Hospital, </institution></institution-wrap>&#x00130;stanbul, Turkey </aff><aff id="Aff2"><label>2</label><institution-wrap><institution-id institution-id-type="ROR">https://ror.org/00nwc4v84</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="GRID">grid.414850.c</institution-id><institution-id institution-id-type="ISNI">0000 0004 0642 8921</institution-id><institution>Anesthesia Department, </institution><institution>Gaziosmanpa&#x0015f;a Training and Research Hospital, </institution></institution-wrap>Gaziosmanpa&#x0015f;a, Istanbul, Turkey </aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>4</day><month>8</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>4</day><month>8</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="collection"><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>25</volume><elocation-id>393</elocation-id><history><date date-type="received"><day>6</day><month>5</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>17</day><month>7</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; The Author(s) 2025</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p><bold>Open Access</bold> This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article&#x02019;s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article&#x02019;s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link>.</license-p></license></permissions><abstract id="Abs1"><sec><title>Background</title><p id="Par1">Quadratus lumborum block (QLB) and modified thoracoabdominal nerves block through perichondrial approach (m-TAPA) can be used for postoperative analgesia in laparoscopic cholecystectomies (LC). The aim of this study is to evaluate the analgesic efficacy of anterior QLB and m-TAPA during the first 24 hours postoperatively.</p></sec><sec><title>Methods</title><p id="Par2">This study was designed as a randomized, prospective, single-blinded trial. Preoperatively, patients were divided into two groups: Group QLB (n=55) and Group m-TAPA (n=53). The anterior QLB was applied to Group QLB, while the m-TAPA was applied to the other group. After the blocks were administered, the dermatome areas covered at the 20th and 30th minutes were evaluated using a cold test. Intraoperative remifentanil consumption was recorded, while Numerical Rating Scale scores at rest (rNRS) and during movement (dNRS), opioid consumption, time to first rescue analgesic use, and complications were evaluated during the first 24 postoperative hours.</p></sec><sec><title>Results</title><p id="Par3">There was no statistically significant difference between the groups in terms of total analgesic consumption during the first 24 hours, rNRS and dNRS values at hours 1, 4, 8, 12, and 24, time to first rescue analgesic use, intraoperative remifentanil consumption and complications. When comparing dermatome involvement between the groups, it was observed that dermatome coverage in the m-TAPA group was mostly distributed in the T7-T10 dermatomes, while in the QLB group, it was mostly distributed in the T10-L1 dermatomes.</p></sec><sec><title>Conclusion</title><p id="Par4">We believe that both anterior QLB and m-TAPA can be used as part of opioid-free multimodal analgesia for postoperative analgesia during the first 24 hours in LC.</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group xml:lang="en"><title>Keywords</title><kwd>Postoperative analgesia</kwd><kwd>Nerve block</kwd><kwd>Dermatomes</kwd><kwd>Quadratus lumborum block</kwd><kwd>Intercostal nerves</kwd><kwd>Laparoscopic cholecystectomy</kwd></kwd-group><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>issue-copyright-statement</meta-name><meta-value>&#x000a9; BioMed Central Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><sec id="Sec1"><title>Introduction</title><p id="Par5">Laparoscopic cholecystectomy (LC) is one of the most commonly performed surgical techniques. Although it causes less pain intensity compared to open cholecystectomy, patients may still experience moderate to severe pain in the postoperative period due to somatic pain at trocar insertion sites, visceral pain caused by surgical manipulation of the gallbladder, and secondary effects of abdominal insufflation with CO&#x02082; [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR2">2</xref>].</p><p id="Par6">For postoperative analgesia in LC, opioid analgesics have traditionally been used for many years and are still in use. However, in recent years, with advancements in ultrasonography technology and the development of novel truncal blocks, techniques such as Quadratus Lumborum Block (QLB) and modified thoracoabdominal nerve block through perichondrial approach (m-TAPA) have been utilized as part of multimodal analgesia to avoid opioid-related side effects under the concept of opioid-free analgesia [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR3">3</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR6">6</xref>]. Additionally, these blocks are considered applicable for postoperative analgesia in other surgical procedures involving the abdomen.</p><p id="Par7">It has been reported in the literature that m-TAPA can provide somatic analgesia in the T6-T12 dermatomes, whereas QLB can provide both somatic and visceral analgesia in the T7-L2 dermatomes [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR5">5</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR7">7</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref>]. We hypothesized that anterior QLB would provide superior analgesia compared to m-TAPA in LC.</p><p id="Par8">To date, no study in the literature has compared the postoperative analgesic efficacy of Aanterior QLB and m-TAPA in LC.</p><p id="Par9">The aim of this study is to compare the postoperative analgesic efficacy of QLB and m-TAPA applied for postoperative analgesia in LC.</p></sec><sec id="Sec2"><title>Materials and methods</title><sec id="Sec3"><title>Study design</title><p id="Par10">This study was designed as a prospective, randomized, single-blinded trial and was conducted and reported in accordance with the CONSORT (Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials) guidelines. Ethical approval was obtained from the Clinical Research Ethics Committee of Gaziosmanpa&#x0015f;a Training and Research Hospital with the registration number 26.04.2023/57, and the study was registered in the ClinicalTrials database with the number NCT06540586.</p><p id="Par11">The study was conducted in the general surgery operating rooms of Gaziosmanpa&#x0015f;a Training and Research Hospital between May 1, 2024, and October 1, 2024, in accordance with the principles of the Helsinki Declaration.</p><p id="Par12">This randomized controlled trial was conducted and reported in accordance with the CONSORT (Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials) guidelines.</p></sec><sec id="Sec4"><title>Participants</title><p id="Par13">Patients aged&#x02009;&#x02265;&#x02009;18 years with ASA I-II risk status and scheduled for elective LC were included in the study. Patients with known local anesthetic allergy, a history of infection in the application area, prior abdominal surgery, coagulation disorders, a history of chronic analgesic or opioid use, neurological or psychological disorders, communication difficulties, ASA III status, or BMI&#x02009;&#x0003e;&#x02009;35&#x000a0;kg/m&#x000b2; were excluded from the study.</p><p id="Par14">All patients were provided with detailed verbal and written information, and written informed consent was obtained.</p></sec><sec id="Sec5"><title>Grouping and randomization</title><p id="Par15">Patients receiving anterior QLB were assigned to the QLB group, while those receiving m-TAPA were assigned to the m-TAPA group. The randomisation sequence was generated using a computer program and stored in sealed, numbered envelopes. Each participant was assigned to a group by opening the next envelope in sequence. All block procedures were performed by the same experienced anesthesiologist.</p><p id="Par16">The blocks were performed 30&#x000a0;min preoperatively in the block room under standard monitoring and aseptic conditions.</p></sec><sec id="Sec6"><title>QLB procedure</title><p id="Par17">In the QLB group, patients were placed in the lateral position. A convex ultrasound (US) probe (2&#x02013;6&#x000a0;MHz) (MyLabseven; Esaote Europe, Netherlands) was placed between the subcostal area and the iliac crest. The quadratus lumborum and psoas major muscles, as well as the transverse processes of the L4 vertebra, were visualized in the midaxillary line. Using the in-plane technique, a 22G 100&#x000a0;mm peripheral nerve block needle (Stimuplex<sup>&#x000ae;</sup> Ultra; B. Braun Melsungen AG, Germany) was advanced into the subfascial space anterior to the quadratus lumborum muscle. Hydrodissection with 1&#x02013;2 mL of 0.9% saline was performed to confirm the location, followed by the administration of 20 mL of 0.25% bupivacaine. The procedure was repeated on the contralateral side.</p></sec><sec id="Sec7"><title>m-TAPA procedure</title><p id="Par18">In the m-TAPA group, patients were placed in the supine position with the head elevated at a 20-degree angle. A linear US probe (3&#x02013;13&#x000a0;MHz) (MyLabseven; Esaote Europe, Netherlands) was placed longitudinally at the 9th&#x02013;10th costochondral junction. An 8&#x000a0;cm 22G needle (Stimuplex<sup>&#x000ae;</sup> A, B. Braun, Melsungen, Germany) was advanced in-plane from caudal to cranial direction onto the fascia of the transversus abdominis muscle below the costochondral junction. After confirming the location using hydrodissection, 20 mL of 0.25% bupivacaine was administered. The same procedure was repeated on the contralateral side.</p></sec><sec id="Sec8"><title>Dermatomal analyses</title><p id="Par19">Following the block application, a blinded anesthesiologist performed a cold test using a cold ice pack at the 20th and 30th minutes. Before testing the dermatomal areas, patients were familiarized with the sensation of cold by applying the ice pack to the deltoid muscle on the shoulder region. Subsequently, dermatomal areas were assessed sequentially from the T4 dermatome to the L2 dermatome along the midclavicular line, starting with the right side and then the left. To prevent localized warming of the ice pack, its surface contacting the skin was replaced after testing each dermatome. The cold test involved applying the ice pack to each dermatome for 2&#x000a0;s. The absence or significant reduction of cold sensation was interpreted as sensory cutaneous block and dermatome involvement, which was recorded. Patients with no observed dermatome involvement at the 30th minute were excluded from the study. Patients with documented dermatome involvement at the 30th minute were transferred to the operating room for surgery.</p></sec><sec id="Sec9"><title>General anesthesia application</title><p id="Par20">All patients were administered a standard general anesthesia technique. Anesthesia induction included intravenous administration of midazolam 0.04&#x000a0;mg/kg, lidocaine 0.5&#x000a0;mg/kg, propofol 2&#x000a0;mg/kg, fentanyl 1&#x000a0;&#x000b5;g/kg, and rocuronium 0.6&#x000a0;mg/kg. Anesthesia was maintained with sevoflurane and remifentanil. A remifentanil infusion at 0.05&#x02013;2&#x000a0;&#x000b5;g/kg/min was used to keep hemodynamic parameters within 20% of the baseline values. The same surgical team performed LC using the standard 4-port technique with carbon dioxide pneumoperitoneum. The pneumoperitoneum pressure was maintained below 12 mmHg throughout the operation.</p><p id="Par21">At the end of the operation, all patients were administered 1&#x000a0;g of paracetamol, 1&#x000a0;mg/kg of tramadol, and 0.1&#x000a0;mg/kg of ondansetron intravenously. At the end of the surgery, the residual neuromuscular blockade was reversed with 50&#x000a0;&#x000b5;g/kg of intravenous neostigmine and 0.3&#x02013;0.5&#x000a0;mg of atropine.</p><p id="Par22">The intraoperative remifentanil consumption of all patients was recorded. At the end of the surgery, all patients were transferred to the Post-Anesthesia Care Unit (PACU). Patients who were monitored in the PACU for 30&#x000a0;min and achieved a Modified Aldrete Score of &#x02265;&#x02009;9 were transferred to the ward. Patients who experienced severe intraoperative hemodynamic instability, significant bleeding or conversion to open surgery were excluded from the study.</p></sec><sec id="Sec10"><title>Postoperative analgesia regimen and outcomes</title><p id="Par23">Postoperative routine analgesia was provided with 1&#x000a0;g of paracetamol four times daily. If the resting NRS was &#x02265;&#x02009;4, 100&#x000a0;mg of intravenous tramadol was administered as rescue analgesia. A maximum of 3 doses were allowed within 24&#x000a0;h, with at least 6&#x000a0;h between doses.</p><p id="Par24">All patients were visited at postoperative hours 1, 4, 8, 12, and 24 by a blinded anesthesiologist to evaluate the blocks applied. The following parameters were recorded: patients&#x02019; tramadol consumption, time to first rescue analgesic use, resting NRS (rNRS), dynamic NRS (dNRS) values, and side effects such as nausea, vomiting, and shoulder pain. The values of NRS were defined as follows: 0 for no pain and 10 for the most severe intolerable pain.</p><p id="Par25">Patients who developed any allergic reactions or complications during postoperative follow-up or who did not adhere to the rescue analgesia regimen were excluded from the study.</p><p id="Par26">The primary aim of this study is to compare the 24-hour postoperative opioid consumption between anterior QLB and m-TAPA in LC.</p><p id="Par27">The secondary aim is to compare the NRS scores at postoperative hours 1, 4, 8, 12, and 24; dermatomal involvement at the 20th and 30th minutes after block application; time to first rescue analgesic use; intraoperative total remifentanil consumption; and side effects such as postoperative nausea, vomiting, and shoulder pain between the groups.</p></sec><sec id="Sec11"><title>Sample size and statistical analyses</title><p id="Par28">Based on our preliminary data (mean [&#x000b1;&#x02009;standard deviation] 24-hour tramadol consumption: 54.1&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;42.0&#x000a0;mg in the QLB group and 75.3&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;32.5&#x000a0;mg in the m-TAPA group) and using G*Power with a type 1 error of 0.05 and power of 0.80, we determined that a sample size of 51 in each group was required to detect a significant difference [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR9">9</xref>]. To account for potential dropouts, this was increased to 60 patients in each group.</p><p id="Par29">The data analysis was performed using IBM SPSS Statistics 26. Categorical variables were presented as frequencies (numbers, percentages), and numerical variables were presented using descriptive statistics (mean, standard deviation, median, minimum, maximum). The normality of numerical data was assessed using the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test, which revealed that some variables were normally distributed, while others were not. Therefore, both parametric and non-parametric methods were used. Differences between two independent groups were analyzed using the Independent Samples T-Test and Mann-Whitney U Test. Differences between more than two dependent numerical variables were evaluated using Friedman Analysis. When a difference was detected in the Friedman Analysis, Bonferroni correction was applied to identify the source of the difference. Relationships between categorical variables were assessed using the Chi-Square test. A P-value of &#x0003c;&#x02009;0.05 was considered statistically significant for all statistical tests.</p></sec></sec><sec id="Sec12"><title>Results</title><p id="Par30">A total of 135 patients were evaluated for this study. Six patients declined to participate, four patients were excluded due to being in the ASA-III risk group, and five patients had a history of prior abdominal surgery. Randomization was performed with 60 patients assigned to each group. In the QLB group, one patient exhibited no dermatome involvement, one patient underwent conversion to laparotomy, and three patients were excluded due to noncompliance during ward follow-up. In the m-TAPA group, two patients exhibited no dermatome involvement at the 30th minute, two patients underwent conversion to laparotomy, and three patients were excluded due to noncompliance during ward follow-up. As a result, a total of 108 patients were analyzed, with 55 in the QLB group and 53 in the m-TAPA group (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig1" ref-type="fig">1</xref>).</p><p id="Par31">
<fig id="Fig1"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Flow diagram of the study (CONSORT flow diagram)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="12871_2025_3281_Fig1_HTML" id="MO1"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par32">The demographic data were found to be similar between the groups (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="Tab1" ref-type="table">1</xref>).</p><p id="Par33">
<table-wrap id="Tab1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Comparison of demographic data and operational durations among groups</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="2"/><th align="left" colspan="2">Group m-TAPA<break/>(<italic>n</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;53)</th><th align="left" colspan="2">Group QLB<break/>(<italic>n</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;55)</th><th align="left" rowspan="2">
<italic>p</italic>
</th></tr><tr><th align="left">
<italic>n</italic>
</th><th align="left">%</th><th align="left">
<italic>n</italic>
</th><th align="left">%</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Gender</td><td align="left"/><td align="left"/><td align="left"/><td align="left"/><td align="left" rowspan="3">0.9</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;Female</td><td align="left">39</td><td align="left">73.6</td><td align="left">41</td><td align="left">74.5</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;Male</td><td align="left">14</td><td align="left">26.4</td><td align="left">14</td><td align="left">25.5</td></tr><tr><td align="left"/><td align="left" colspan="2">Mean&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;SD</td><td align="left" colspan="2">Mean&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;SD</td><td align="left"/></tr><tr><td align="left">Age (yr)</td><td align="left" colspan="2">47.08&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;13.31</td><td align="left" colspan="2">42.73&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;0.74</td><td align="left">0.1</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><p>BMI</p><p>(kg/m<sup>2</sup>)</p></td><td align="left" colspan="2">28.84&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;4.26</td><td align="left" colspan="2">28.41&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;4.34</td><td align="left">0,6</td></tr><tr><td align="left"/><td align="left" colspan="2"><p>Median</p><p>(Min-Max)</p></td><td align="left" colspan="2"><p>Median</p><p>(Min-Max)</p></td><td align="left"/></tr><tr><td align="left"><p>Operation Time</p><p>(min)</p></td><td align="left" colspan="2"><p>52.5</p><p>(25&#x02013;130)</p></td><td align="left" colspan="2"><p>49</p><p>(15&#x02013;150)</p></td><td align="left">0.3</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Values are presented as mean&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;SD or median (minimum&#x02013;maximum)</p><p><italic>Group QLB</italic> Quadratus Lumborum Block group, <italic>Group m-TAPA</italic> Modified Thoracoabdominal Plane Block group, <italic>BMI</italic> Body Mass Index, <italic>SD</italic> Standard Deviation</p><p>&#x000a0;Normality of continuous variables was assessed using the Kolmogorov&#x02013;Smirnov test</p><p>Independent samples t-test was used for normally distributed variables (age, BMI)</p><p>Mann&#x02013;Whitney U test was applied for non-normally distributed variable (operation time)</p><p>Categorical variables were compared using the Chi-square test</p><p>Statistical significance was set at <italic>p</italic>&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.05</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap>
</p><p id="Par34">When comparing total tramadol consumption during the first 24 postoperative hours, time to first rescue analgesic use, and intraoperative total remifentanil consumption, no statistically significant differences were observed between the two groups (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="Tab2" ref-type="table">2</xref>).</p><p id="Par35">
<table-wrap id="Tab2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Comparison of intraoperative remifentanil consumption, total analgesic consumption, and time to first analgesic use between Grup</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="2"/><th align="left" colspan="2">Group m-TAPA<break/>(<italic>n</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;53)</th><th align="left" colspan="2">Group QLB<break/>(<italic>n</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;55)</th><th align="left" rowspan="2">
<italic>p</italic>
</th></tr><tr><th align="left">Mean&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;SD</th><th align="left">Median<break/>(Min - Max)</th><th align="left">Mean&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;SD</th><th align="left">Median<break/>(Min - Max)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Intraoperative Remifentanil Consumption (mcg/kg)</td><td align="left">2.63&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;2.59</td><td align="left"><p>1.94</p><p>(0.47&#x02013;15.55)</p></td><td align="left">3.24&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.00</td><td align="left"><p>2.32</p><p>(0.52&#x02013;15.46)</p></td><td align="left">0.181</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><p>Total Tramadol Consumption</p><p>(mg/day)</p></td><td align="left">58.49&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;81.89</td><td align="left"><p>0</p><p>(0-300)</p></td><td align="left">37.45&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;54.17</td><td align="left"><p>0</p><p>(0-200)</p></td><td align="left">0.281</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Time to First Rescue Analgesia (hour)</td><td align="left">2.06&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;2.27</td><td align="left"><p>1</p><p>(0&#x02013;9)</p></td><td align="left">2.37&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.79</td><td align="left"><p>1</p><p>(0&#x02013;14)</p></td><td align="left">0.222</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Values are presented as mean&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;SD and median (minimum&#x02013;maximum)</p><p><italic>Group QLB</italic> Quadratus Lumborum Block group, <italic>Group m-TAPA </italic>Modified Thoracoabdominal Plane Block group, <italic>SD</italic> Standard Deviation</p><p>Since the variables did not follow a normal distribution as assessed by the Kolmogorov&#x02013;Smirnov test, both mean&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;standard deviation and median (min&#x02013;max) values are presented for each group</p><p>Between-group comparisons were conducted using the Mann&#x02013;Whitney U test</p><p><italic>p</italic>&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.05</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap>
</p><p id="Par36">When postoperative analgesia request times were analyzed using Kaplan-Meier survival analysis with a One Minus Survival graph, it was observed that 40% of patients in the QLB group had earlier analgesia requests, but other patients in this group demonstrated longer analgesia durations compared to the m-TAPA group. Analgesia requests in the m-TAPA group occurred earlier compared to the QLB group (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>).</p><p id="Par37">
<fig id="Fig2"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>Comparison of Time to First Analgesic Request Between Group m-TAPA and Group QLB: Kaplan-Meier Analysis</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="12871_2025_3281_Fig2_HTML" id="MO2"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par38">When rNRS and dNRS scores at postoperative hours 1, 4, 8, 12, and 24 were compared between the groups, both rNRS and dNRS scores were similar (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="Tab3" ref-type="table">3</xref>).</p><p id="Par39">
<table-wrap id="Tab3"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Comparison of rNRS and dNRS values between groups</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="2"/><th align="left">Group m-TAPA<break/>(<italic>n</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;53)</th><th align="left">Group QLB<break/>(<italic>n</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;55)</th><th align="left" rowspan="2">
<italic>p</italic>
</th></tr><tr><th align="left">Median<break/>(Min - Max)</th><th align="left">Median<break/>(Min - Max)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">rNRS 1.hour</td><td align="left">2 (0&#x02013;7)</td><td align="left">2 (0&#x02013;8)</td><td align="left">0.670</td></tr><tr><td align="left">rNRS 4. hour</td><td align="left">2 (0&#x02013;5)</td><td align="left">2 (0&#x02013;5)</td><td align="left">0.820</td></tr><tr><td align="left">rNRS 8. hour</td><td align="left">2 (0&#x02013;5)</td><td align="left">2 (0&#x02013;5)</td><td align="left">0.715</td></tr><tr><td align="left">rNRS 12.hour</td><td align="left">2 (0&#x02013;3)</td><td align="left">1 (0&#x02013;4)</td><td align="left">0.171</td></tr><tr><td align="left">rNRS 24.hour</td><td align="left">1 (0&#x02013;2)</td><td align="left">1 (0&#x02013;3)</td><td align="left">0.100</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Fr; p</td><td align="left">41.262; 0.000*</td><td align="left">32.271; 0.000*</td><td align="left"/></tr><tr><td align="left">Difference (Bonferroni)</td><td align="left">24&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;1,4,8</td><td align="left"><p>24&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;1,4</p><p>12&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;1</p></td><td align="left"/></tr><tr><td align="left">dNRS 1. hour</td><td align="left">3 (0&#x02013;8)</td><td align="left">3 (0&#x02013;8)</td><td align="left">0.928</td></tr><tr><td align="left">dNRS 4. hour</td><td align="left">3 (0&#x02013;6)</td><td align="left">3 (0&#x02013;7)</td><td align="left">0.410</td></tr><tr><td align="left">dNRS 8. hour</td><td align="left">3 (0&#x02013;6)</td><td align="left">3 (0&#x02013;7)</td><td align="left">0.909</td></tr><tr><td align="left">dNRS 12.hour</td><td align="left">2 (0&#x02013;5)</td><td align="left">2 (1&#x02013;5)</td><td align="left">0.974</td></tr><tr><td align="left">dNRS 24.hour</td><td align="left">2 (0&#x02013;3)</td><td align="left">2 (0&#x02013;4)</td><td align="left">0.260</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Fr; p</td><td align="left">43,324; 0.000*</td><td align="left">38,929; 0.000*</td><td align="left"/></tr><tr><td align="left">Difference (Bonferroni)</td><td align="left"><p>24&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;1,4,8</p><p>12&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;1</p></td><td align="left"><p>24&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;1,4,8</p><p>12&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;1</p></td><td align="left"/></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Values are presented as median (minimum&#x02013;maximum)</p><p><italic>Group QLB</italic> Quadratus Lumborum Block group, <italic>Group m-TAPA</italic> Modified Thoracoabdominal Plane Block group, <italic>rNRS </italic>Resting Numeric Rating Scale, <italic>dNRS</italic> Dynamic Numeric Rating Scale, <italic>SD</italic> Standard Deviation</p><p>Between-group comparisons were performed using the Mann&#x02013;Whitney U test</p><p>Within-group repeated measurements were evaluated using Friedman&#x02019;s test</p><p>Bonferroni correction was applied to determine the time points responsible for significant differences</p><p>*=<italic>p</italic>&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.05</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap>
</p><p id="Par40">At the 20th minute, a greater number of patients in the m-TAPA group exhibited sensory block in the bilateral T6, T7, T8, T9, and T10 dermatomes. Conversely, bilateral involvement of the T11, T12, and L1 dermatomes was observed in a greater number of patients in the QLB group (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="Tab4" ref-type="table">4</xref>).</p><p id="Par41">
