td><td align="left">1.8</td><td align="left">-</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T5</td><td align="left">1</td><td align="left">1.9</td><td align="left">0</td><td align="left">0.0</td><td align="left">-</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T6</td><td align="left">15</td><td align="left">28.3</td><td align="left">4</td><td align="left">7.3</td><td align="left">0.004*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T7</td><td align="left">42</td><td align="left">79.2</td><td align="left">13</td><td align="left">23.6</td><td align="left">0.000*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T8</td><td align="left">48</td><td align="left">90.6</td><td align="left">15</td><td align="left">27.3</td><td align="left">0.000*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T9</td><td align="left">38</td><td align="left">71.7</td><td align="left">24</td><td align="left">43.6</td><td align="left">0.003*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T10</td><td align="left">37</td><td align="left">69.8</td><td align="left">50</td><td align="left">90.9</td><td align="left">0.006*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T11</td><td align="left">14</td><td align="left">26.4</td><td align="left">46</td><td align="left">83.6</td><td align="left">0.000*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T12</td><td align="left">9</td><td align="left">17.0</td><td align="left">46</td><td align="left">83.6</td><td align="left">0.000*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;L1</td><td align="left">2</td><td align="left">3.8</td><td align="left">42</td><td align="left">76.4</td><td align="left">0.000*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;L2</td><td align="left">0</td><td align="left">0.0</td><td align="left">10</td><td align="left">18.2</td><td align="left">-</td></tr><tr><td align="left" colspan="6">Left 30.minute</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T4</td><td align="left">0</td><td align="left">0.0</td><td align="left">0</td><td align="left">0.0</td><td align="left">-</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T5</td><td align="left">1</td><td align="left">1.9</td><td align="left">0</td><td align="left">0.0</td><td align="left">-</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T6</td><td align="left">14</td><td align="left">26.4</td><td align="left">4</td><td align="left">7.3</td><td align="left">0.008*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T7</td><td align="left">38</td><td align="left">71.7</td><td align="left">13</td><td align="left">23.6</td><td align="left">0.000*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T8</td><td align="left">42</td><td align="left">79.2</td><td align="left">17</td><td align="left">30.9</td><td align="left">0.000*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T9</td><td align="left">37</td><td align="left">69.8</td><td align="left">26</td><td align="left">47.3</td><td align="left">0.018*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T10</td><td align="left">38</td><td align="left">71.7</td><td align="left">50</td><td align="left">90.9</td><td align="left">0.010*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T11</td><td align="left">14</td><td align="left">26.4</td><td align="left">46</td><td align="left">83.6</td><td align="left">0.000*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;T12</td><td align="left">9</td><td align="left">17.0</td><td align="left">45</td><td align="left">81.8</td><td align="left">0.000*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;L1</td><td align="left">3</td><td align="left">5.7</td><td align="left">40</td><td align="left">72.7</td><td align="left">0.000*</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x02003;L2</td><td align="left">0</td><td align="left">0.0</td><td align="left">10</td><td align="left">18.2</td><td align="left">-</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Values are presented as number and percentage (n, %)</p><p><italic>Group QLB</italic> Quadratus Lumborum Block group, <italic>Group m-TAPA</italic> Modified Thoracoabdominal Plane Block group</p><p><italic>T4</italic>&#x000a0;Thoracic 5 dermatome area, <italic>T5</italic>&#x000a0;Thoracic 5 dermatome area, <italic>T6</italic>&#x000a0;Thoracic 6 dermatome area, <italic>T7</italic>&#x000a0;Thoracic 7 dermatome area, <italic>T8</italic>&#x000a0;Thoracic 8 dermatome area, <italic>T9</italic>&#x000a0;Thoracic 9 dermatome area, <italic>T10</italic>&#x000a0;Thoracic 10 dermatome area, <italic>T11</italic>&#x000a0;Thoracic 11 dermatome area, <italic>T12</italic>&#x000a0;Thoracic 12 dermatome area, <italic>L1</italic>&#x000a0;Lumbar 1 dermatome area, <italic>L2 </italic>Lumbar 2 dermatome area</p><p>&#x000a0;<italic>Statistical test</italic> Chi-square test</p><p>*= <italic>p</italic>&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.05</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap>
</p><p id="Par42">At the 30th minute, a greater number of patients in the m-TAPA group exhibited sensory block in the bilateral T6, T7, T8, and T9 dermatomes compared to the QLB group. In contrast, bilateral involvement of the T10, T11, T12, and L1 dermatomes was more frequently observed in the QLB group (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="Tab4" ref-type="table">4</xref>).</p><p id="Par43">When comparing side effects such as nausea, vomiting, and shoulder pain during the first 24 postoperative hours, no significant differences were observed between the groups (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="Tab5" ref-type="table">5</xref>).</p><p id="Par44">
<table-wrap id="Tab5"><label>Table 5</label><caption><p>Comparison of postoperative side effects between the groups</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="2"/><th align="left" colspan="2">Group m-TAPA<break/>(<italic>n</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;53)</th><th align="left" colspan="2">Group QLB<break/>(<italic>n</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;55)</th><th align="left" rowspan="2">
<italic>p</italic>
</th></tr><tr><th align="left">
<italic>n</italic>
</th><th align="left">%</th><th align="left">
<italic>n</italic>
</th><th align="left">%</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Nausea</td><td align="left">20</td><td align="left">37.7</td><td align="left">21</td><td align="left">38.2</td><td align="left">0.962</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Vomiting</td><td align="left">12</td><td align="left">22.6</td><td align="left">15</td><td align="left">27.3</td><td align="left">0.578</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Shoulder pain</td><td align="left">12</td><td align="left">22.6</td><td align="left">15</td><td align="left">27.3</td><td align="left">0.578</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Values are presented as number and percentage (n, %)</p><p>&#x000a0;<italic>Group QLB</italic> Quadratus Lumborum Block group, <italic>Group m-TAPA</italic> Modified Thoracoabdominal Plane Block group&#x000a0;</p><p><italic>Statistical test</italic>&#x000a0;Chi-square test</p><p>p&#x02009;<italic>&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.05</italic></p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap>
</p></sec><sec id="Sec13"><title>Discussion</title><p id="Par45">The results of this study indicate that anterior QLB and m-TAPA applied for postoperative analgesia in LC have similar analgesic efficacy during the first 24 postoperative hours. Both truncal block techniques demonstrated comparable NRS scores, total analgesic consumption, time to first rescue analgesia, intraoperative remifentanil consumption, and side effects during the first 24&#x000a0;h postoperatively. When the dermatomal distribution of both block techniques was evaluated, T7&#x02013;T10 dermatomal involvement was predominantly observed in patients who received the m-TAPA block, whereas T10&#x02013;L1 involvement was predominantly observed in those who received the QLB.</p><p id="Par46">Truncal blocks, as part of multimodal analgesia following LC, provide not only effective and prolonged analgesia but may also improve clinical recovery. Although this study focused on opioid consumption as the primary outcome, reducing opioid use can yield additional benefits, such as fewer opioid-related side effects, enhanced patient comfort, earlier mobilization, and shorter hospital stays. Therefore, the growing use of truncal blocks may contribute significantly to enhanced postoperative recovery and healthcare efficiency.</p><p id="Par47">QLB can be utilized for postoperative analgesia in surgeries involving the lower thoracic region, abdomen, lower abdomen, retroperitoneal area, and inguinal region. QLB was first described by Blanco in 2007 [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR10">10</xref>]. Based on cadaveric and radiological imaging studies, it is believed that the local anesthetic spreads to the thoracic paravertebral space and intercostal areas, surrounding the somatic nerves and thoracic sympathetic trunk. Therefore, in addition to providing somatic analgesia, QLB may also offer visceral analgesic effects [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR7">7</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR8">8</xref>].</p><p id="Par48">QLB have been shown in the literature to provide effective analgesia for abdominal surgeries [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR11">11</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR13">13</xref>]. Okmen et al. [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR4">4</xref>] reported lower visual analog scale scores and reduced tramadol consumption in patients undergoing LC with posterior QLB compared to a control group. Furthermore, QLB have also demonstrated efficacy in lower abdominal procedures, such as cesarean sections and inguinal hernia repairs [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR14">14</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR15">15</xref>]. Our findings regarding the distribution of sensory block are consistent with these results, as more extensive involvement was observed in the lower abdominal dermatomes.</p><p id="Par49">The m-TAPA, described by Tulgar et al. [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">16</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR17">17</xref>] is a fascial plane block reported to provide somatic analgesia in surgical procedures involving the abdomen. It is believed to provide somatic analgesia in the T5-T12 dermatomal areas and can be used for all abdominal procedures. Unlike transversus abdominis plane (TAP) blocks, it has been shown to block both the lateral and medial cutaneous branches of the thoracoabdominal nerves. It can be performed using a perichondral approach below the 9th-10th costochondral junction. Since this area is the pathway for thoracoabdominal nerves, it is thought that local anesthetics injected here provide abdominal analgesia by blocking these nerves.</p><p id="Par50">&#x000c7;ift&#x000e7;i et al. [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR18">18</xref>] reported in cadaveric studies that dye injected below the costochondral junction onto the fascia of the transversus abdominis muscle spread to the T4&#x02013;T11/12 dermatomes, the TAP, the fascia over the rectus abdominis muscle, and the regions above and below the external and internal oblique muscles. Other cadaveric studies have similarly shown that the spread is primarily distributed within the T8&#x02013;T11 dermatomes and is predominantly localized to the anterior cutaneous region [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR19">19</xref>, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR20">20</xref>].</p><p id="Par51">In studies comparing m-TAPA performed using the perichondral approach in LC to control groups or wound site infiltration, patients who received m-TAPA were reported to have lower rescue analgesic requirements, reduced NRS scores, and fewer side effects [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR21">21</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR24">24</xref>].</p><p id="Par52">Gen&#x000e7; et al. [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR5">5</xref>], in their study involving 24 patients undergoing LC, compared dermatomal involvement and regression rates between patients who received m-TAPA and those who received external oblique intercostal plane blocks. They reported that median sensory cutaneous blockade rates were higher in the m-TAPA group. Complete (100%) involvement of the T7&#x02013;T10 dermatomes was observed, while the T5 and T12 dermatomes showed lower involvement, and no involvement was noted in the L1 dermatome. These findings were consistent with the dermatomal involvement observed following m-TAPA in the present study. In the present study, sensory block in the T7&#x02013;T10 dermatomes was observed in approximately 70&#x02013;90% of patients in the m-TAPA group at the 30th minute. Dermatomal spread to the T6&#x02013;T12 dermatomes was also observed, but in fewer patients. Involvement of T5 dermatome was observed in only one patient, and L1 dermatome involvement was limited to three patients. The L1 involvement was considered to be potentially associated with fibers originating from the T12 level.</p><p id="Par53">A comprehensive review by Tanaka et al. [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR25">25</xref>] evaluating m-TAPA reported that patients who received the block predominantly exhibited involvement of the anterior cutaneous branches (T7&#x02013;T11). Involvement of the lateral cutaneous branches was observed, particularly in the T8&#x02013;T10 dermatomes, though at a lower rate compared to the anterior branches. They further reported that somatic analgesia in the anterior cutaneous branches (T7&#x02013;T11) persisted for up to 14&#x000a0;h, making it effective for upper abdominal laparoscopic procedures. Involvement of T12, however, was not consistently observed. In the present study, the cold test was performed along the midclavicular line, as this area is considered to more accurately represent the anterior cutaneous distribution, which is predominantly affected following m-TAPA blocks. Accordingly, dermatomal sensory involvement patterns consistent with those reported in the anterior cutaneous branches were observed.</p><p id="Par54">In the QLB group, dermatomal involvement was predominantly observed at the T10&#x02013;L1 levels, with sensory blockade detected in approximately 70&#x02013;90% of patients at the 30th minute. Involvement extending cranially up to the T6 dermatome and caudally down to the L2 dermatome was also observed, although in fewer patients. T6 dermatome involvement was observed in only four patients. At the 20th minute, T10 dermatome involvement was observed in a greater number of patients in the m-TAPA group. However, by the 30th minute, it was observed in more patients in the QLB group. This finding suggests that sensory blockade at the T10 dermatome may occur earlier in the m-TAPA group than in the QLB group. We hypothesize that this difference may be related to the more rapid spread of local anesthetic within the TAP plane.</p><p id="Par55">In the present study, the analgesic efficacy of QLB, a block associated with potential visceral analgesic effects, was compared with that of m-TAPA, which is known to provide only somatic analgesia but is expected to result in dermatomal involvement in the upper abdomen. Although QLB is theoretically considered superior in providing visceral analgesia due to its proximity to central neural structures, m-TAPA&#x02014;despite being known to produce only somatic blockade during LC surgeries&#x02014;was found to exhibit comparable analgesic efficacy. These findings suggest that in LC procedures, somatic pain originating from trocar insertion sites may be more predominant than visceral pain [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR26">26</xref>].</p><p id="Par56">One advantage of m-TAPA over QLB is that it can be performed in the supine position. In contrast, QLB must be performed in the lateral decubitus position, which may require additional personnel and time.</p><p id="Par57">Zang et al. [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR27">27</xref>] reported quadriceps weakness in one patient following QLB administration for inguinal hernia surgery. In contrast, no quadriceps weakness was observed in any of the patients in our study. We believe that this difference may be related to the variation in local anesthetic concentration, as Zang et al. used 20 mL of 0.5% ropivacaine, whereas we used 20 mL of 0.25% bupivacaine.</p><p id="Par58">In both block techniques, approximately 50% of patients did not require rescue analgesia within the first 24&#x000a0;h. The mean postoperative total analgesic consumption was 58&#x000a0;mg in the m-TAPA group and 37&#x000a0;mg in the QLB group, with no statistically significant difference. This suggests that both techniques provide sufficient analgesic efficacy. As opioid consumption did not significantly differ between the groups, opioid-related adverse effects such as nausea, and vomiting occurred at similar rates. Postoperative analgesia request times were evaluated using Kaplan&#x02013;Meier analysis. Although 40% of patients in the QLB group requested analgesia early, the remaining patients required analgesia over a longer duration compared to those in the m-TAPA group. In contrast, analgesia requests in the m-TAPA group were observed to occur earlier than in the QLB group, indicating that the analgesic effect of m-TAPA may be shorter in duration, leading to an earlier need for rescue analgesia. This observation is consistent with our finding that sensory blockade in the T10 dermatome occurred earlier in the m-TAPA group than in the QLB group.</p><p id="Par59">The limitation of our study was our inability to analyze postoperative dermatomal areas. This was because the data might not have been reliable, as the patients were sedated and under the influence of analgesic medications following general anesthesia. Additionally, since patients at our hospital are typically discharged at the 24th hour after LC, we were only able to perform follow-up within the first 24&#x000a0;h. A longer follow-up period could have provided better insight into the potential for chronicization of postoperative pain, thereby adding more value to the choice of truncal block. Although sensory blockade was evaluated as meticulously as possible in this study, assessment using the cold test remains inherently subjective. The evaluation was performed only along the midclavicular line. Given that lateral cutaneous nerve involvement is not typically expected with m-TAPA, and considering that trocar entry sites are primarily located within the anterior cutaneous nerve innervation zone, assessment along the midaxillary line was not conducted. Although the time to first analgesic demand appeared shorter in the m-TAPA group compared to the QLB group, this difference did not reach statistical significance. Furthermore, the study was not powered to detect differences in this specific parameter, which limits the strength of this finding. Future studies with a larger sample size and a dedicated power analysis for this outcome are warranted.</p><p id="Par60">In summary, we believe that both anterior QLB and m-TAPA procedures can provide effective postoperative analgesia in LC.</p></sec></body><back><fn-group><fn><p><bold>Publisher&#x02019;s Note</bold></p><p>Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.</p></fn></fn-group><ack><title>Acknowledgements</title><p>We would like to thank Omer Kaplan for his contributions to the statistical evaluation of this study.</p></ack><notes notes-type="author-contribution"><title>Authors&#x02019; contributions</title><p>Authors&#x02019; contributions: Data collection: SS, OO, TA, FE. Data analysis: SS, DGM, OO, TA, FE. Writing: SS, DGM, OO, TA, FE. Revising: SS, DGM, OO, TA, FE. Study design: SS, DGM, OO, TA, FE. Patient recruitment: SS, TA. All authors contributed equally to this work. The author(s) read and approved the final manuscript.</p></notes><notes notes-type="funding-information"><title>Funding</title><p>None.</p></notes><notes notes-type="data-availability"><title>Data availability</title><p>The data associated with the paper are not publicly available but are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.</p></notes><notes><title>Declarations</title><notes id="FPar1"><title><bold>Ethics approval and consent to participate</bold></title><p id="Par61">It received approval from the Gaziosmanpa&#x0015f;a Training and Research Hospital Clinical Research Ethics Committee on 26.04.2023 (decision no: 2023/57) and was registered on ClinicalTrials.gov (Registration no: NCT06540586). The study was conducted in the general surgery operating room, following the principles of the Helsinki Declaration, between 01/05/2024 and 01/10/2024. Written and verbal informed consent was obtained from all patients.</p></notes><notes id="FPar2"><title>Consent for publication</title><p id="Par62">Not applicable.</p></notes><notes id="FPar3" notes-type="COI-statement"><title>Competing interests</title><p id="Par63">The authors declare no competing interests.</p></notes></notes><ref-list id="Bib1"><title>References</title><ref id="CR1"><label>1.</label><citation-alternatives><element-citation id="ec-CR1" publication-type="journ